\begin_layout Title
Public Economics I: Field Exam Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Question
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose that you want to estimate the causal effect of receiving a special
 distinction on one's degree.
 To determine what distinction one gets, students take a single exame up
 to two times.
 There is a different cutoff for every level of distinction.
 (i.e., five stars, four stars, three stars, etc.) The cutoffs are not known
 until after everyone takes the test the first time.
 Outcome data are the log of earnings 10 years later.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You want to estimate the effect of receiving five stars versus four stars.
 Which exam score do you use and why? Your choices are the score from the
 1st attempt, the score from the 2nd attempt, the average score, the maximum
 score, etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I would employ a regression discontinuity approach in order to estimate
 the causal effect of receiving a special distinction on one's degree.
 As a result, I would use the score from the first exam because the cutoff
 is not known.
 Though, it is important to note that some people being aware of the cutoff
 does not necessarily mean there is a problem, especially if they are unable
 to manipulate or predict their scores.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What equation would you estimate for part A to estimate the reduced RDD
 impact on earnings?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In order to estimate the reduced-form regression discontunity design (RDD)
 impact on earnings, I would estimate the following equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\log(\mbox{earnings}_{i})=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}1(\mbox{Cutoff\_4 to 5}_{i})+f(\mbox{score}_{i})+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(\mbox{score}_{i})=\mbox{score}_{i}+1(\mbox{Cutoff\_4 to 5}_{i})\times\mbox{score}_{i}
\]

\end_inset

The functional form 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 will be driven by smoothly controlling for the running variable and trying
 to fit the data well.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Since I am only analyzing the scores of the first exam, then I most likely
 have a fuzzy RD.
 With a fuzzy RD, I can also use a 2SLS estimation procedure.
 The first-stage regression is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Prob(5 stars)}_{i}=\gamma_{0}+\gamma_{1}1(\mbox{Cutoff\_4 to 5}_{i})+\theta f(\mbox{score}_{i})+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

and the second-stage follows suit
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\log(\mbox{earnings}_{i})=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\widehat{(\mbox{5 stars}_{i})}+f(\mbox{score}_{i})+\epsilon
\]

\end_inset

In the 2SLS setup, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the coefficient of interest; it is the local average treatment effect
 (LATE) for compliers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part C
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is your coefficient of interest from your equation in part B? If that
 coefficient is 0.02, how do you interpret it? Explain it in layperson-friendly
 terms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The coefficient of interest from my equation in part B is 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Remember, since I am only analyzing the scores of the first exam, then
 I most likely have a fuzzy RD.
 This means that at the cutoff, the discontinuity in the probability of
 treatment is less than one.
 So, having a coefficient of 0.02 means that students just above the cutoff
 will earn 2% more than students just below the cutoff after the first exam.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Suppose the first-stage discontinuity, i.e., the probability of going from
 4 to 5 stars, is 0.5.
 Now, the interpretation of the coefficient in the second-stage regression
 is as follows: if there is a 50% increase in the probability of getting
 5 stars for person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, then his earnings will increase by 2%.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In order to obtain the local average treatment effect for compliers, I must
 take the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

intent to treat
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 estimate, which is 0.02, and divide it by the discontinuity in treatment
 at the cutoff, i.e., the probability of treatment (0.5 in the example above).
 By doing this, I get an estimate of 4% for the local average treatment
 effect.
 Thus, compliers who obtain 5 stars instead of 4 stars will earn 4% more
 in 10 years.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part D
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You do not observe earnings for everyone who tests.
 How can you test whether or not this is going to bias your estimate from
 equation B? Be specific and complete.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Important to note, I can obtain biased estimates in either direction, even
 with random assignment, due to attrition.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
To see if attrition will cause a problem, I can create an indicator that
 signifies whether an individual left the sample after testing, and I put
 this variable on the left hand side of the reduced- form RD equation.
 If there is a discontinuity in the probability of being observed at the
 cutoff, then attrition is a problem.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Another solution is to show that attrition is random.
 To do this, I compare the means of observable variables between the people
 who stayed in the sample with those who did not on either side of the cutoff.
 If the differences are significant, then attrition is a problem.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are other RD robustness tests that I can perform:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For the people who remained in the sample and for those who left early,
 I can show that none of the observable variables possess a discontinuity
 at the cutoff.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Show the density is smooth across the cutoff.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Show the other covariates are smooth across the cutoff.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Show estimates with and without controls.
 If the estimate drastically changes, then there is a problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Part E
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now suppose that your estimates from attempting to do exercises similar
 to part B are too imprecise - that is, you can't precisely estimate the
 impact of 5 versus 4 stars, 4 versus 3, etc.
 So, you instead hope to estimate the impact of having one additional star
 more generally.
 How do you do it? What equation do you estimate? Also, assume that this
 is a fuzzy RD - that some people who are below the cutoff based on their
 score do in earn the higher distinction.
 Carefully define/describe every variable that is in your equation(s).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we have a sharp RD, then I can have earings on the left side, and the
 number of stars on the right side.
 Conditional on test scores, the number of stars should be independent with
 other characteristics that could influence earnings.
 This is an example of conditional independence.
 However, because students are able to take the exam twice, I have a fuzzy
 RD.
 So, the number of stars on the right side is still endogenous, even if
 I condition on test score.
 Here are two solutions to the endogeneity problem:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
I utilize a stacked regression discontuity approach.
 In order to do this, I renorm the cutoff to zero, and I assign every individual
 to his/her closest cutoff.
 Then, the running variable is how far an individual is from the cutoff.
 Finally, I proceed as I normally would when employing a regression discontinuit
y design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
I can also utilize a standard IV approach.
 The IV would be the predicted number of stars an individual receives based
 on his/her score on the first exam.
 The reduced-form IV equation would have earnings on the left side and the
 predicted number of stars on the right side.
 The coefficient on the predicted number of stars would be the estimate
 of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

intent to treat
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 effect.
 I can also obtain the 2SLS estimate, which is just the reweighted 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

intent to treat
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 effect.
\end_layout

